        {
            "content_html": "<img src=\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/159103134?u=61598c0021a74530992fa07526d014fe3564b832&v=4\" width=\"64\" height=\"64\" alt=\"\"/><br/><p dir=\"auto\">Four JavaScript examples document an expected output that the code in the same code block cannot produce. I ran each block with Node 24 (browsers agree for these cases) to check.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\"><strong><code class=\"notranslate\">Date.prototype.setSeconds()</code></strong> — <code class=\"notranslate\">files/en-us/web/javascript/reference/global_objects/date/setseconds/index.md</code></p>\n<div class=\"highlight highlight-source-js notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" dir=\"auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"const event = new Date(&quot;August 19, 1975 23:15:30&quot;);\nevent.setSeconds(42);\nconsole.log(event);\n// Expected output: &quot;Sat Apr 19 1975 23:15:42 GMT+0100 (CET)&quot;   &lt;- says April\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><span class=\"pl-k\">const</span> <span class=\"pl-s1\">event</span> <span class=\"pl-c1\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">new</span> <span class=\"pl-v\">Date</span><span class=\"pl-kos\">(</span><span class=\"pl-s\">\"August 19, 1975 23:15:30\"</span><span class=\"pl-kos\">)</span><span class=\"pl-kos\">;</span>\n<span class=\"pl-s1\">event</span><span class=\"pl-kos\">.</span><span class=\"pl-en\">setSeconds</span><span class=\"pl-kos\">(</span><span class=\"pl-c1\">42</span><span class=\"pl-kos\">)</span><span class=\"pl-kos\">;</span>\n<span class=\"pl-smi\">console</span><span class=\"pl-kos\">.</span><span class=\"pl-en\">log</span><span class=\"pl-kos\">(</span><span class=\"pl-s1\">event</span><span class=\"pl-kos\">)</span><span class=\"pl-kos\">;</span>\n<span class=\"pl-c\">// Expected output: \"Sat Apr 19 1975 23:15:42 GMT+0100 (CET)\"   &lt;- says April</span></pre></div>\n<p dir=\"auto\"><code class=\"notranslate\">setSeconds()</code> does not change the month. August 19, 1975 is a Tuesday, so the line becomes:</p>\n<div class=\"snippet-clipboard-content notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"Tue Aug 19 1975 23:15:42 GMT+0100 (CET)\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><code class=\"notranslate\">Tue Aug 19 1975 23:15:42 GMT+0100 (CET)\n</code></pre></div>\n<p dir=\"auto\"><strong><code class=\"notranslate\">handler.construct()</code></strong> — <code class=\"notranslate\">files/en-us/web/javascript/reference/global_objects/proxy/proxy/construct/index.md</code></p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">The trap logs <code class=\"notranslate\">Creating a ${target.name}</code> and the target is the function <code class=\"notranslate\">Monster</code>, so the console shows <code class=\"notranslate\">Creating a Monster</code> — the existing <code class=\"notranslate\">// Expected output: \"Creating a monster\"</code> has the wrong case.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\"><strong><code class=\"notranslate\">String.prototype.toLocaleLowerCase()</code></strong> — <code class=\"notranslate\">files/en-us/web/javascript/reference/global_objects/string/tolocalelowercase/index.md</code></p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Both logged values are template literals that add a prefix, so the expected output of the first line is <code class=\"notranslate\">\"EN-US: i̇stanbul\"</code> (not <code class=\"notranslate\">\"i̇stanbul\"</code>) and of the second one <code class=\"notranslate\">\"TR: istanbul\"</code> (not <code class=\"notranslate\">\"istanbul\"</code>).</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\"><strong><code class=\"notranslate\">Promise()</code></strong> — <code class=\"notranslate\">files/en-us/web/javascript/reference/global_objects/promise/promise/index.md</code></p>\n<div class=\"highlight highlight-source-js notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" dir=\"auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"console.log(promise1);\n// Expected output: [object Promise]\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><span class=\"pl-smi\">console</span><span class=\"pl-kos\">.</span><span class=\"pl-en\">log</span><span class=\"pl-kos\">(</span><span class=\"pl-s1\">promise1</span><span class=\"pl-kos\">)</span><span class=\"pl-kos\">;</span>\n<span class=\"pl-c\">// Expected output: [object Promise]</span></pre></div>\n<p dir=\"auto\"><code class=\"notranslate\">[object Promise]</code> is the result of <code class=\"notranslate\">String(promise1)</code> (or <code class=\"notranslate\">Object.prototype.toString.call(promise1)</code>); <code class=\"notranslate\">console.log</code> prints the promise object itself, which consoles render as <code class=\"notranslate\">Promise {&lt;pending&gt;}</code> (Chrome) or <code class=\"notranslate\">Promise { &lt;state&gt;: \"pending\" }</code> (Firefox). I used the latter, the form already used on <code class=\"notranslate\">Promise.all()</code>.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Checks: <code class=\"notranslate\">node scripts/front-matter_linter.js</code>, <code class=\"notranslate\">npx markdownlint-cli2</code>, <code class=\"notranslate\">npx prettier -c</code>, <code class=\"notranslate\">npx cspell --config .vscode/cspell.json</code> and <code class=\"notranslate\">npm test</code> — all pass.</p>",
            "url": "https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/46027",
            "title": "fix(js): correct expected output of four JavaScript examples",
